checked.applicability:
Usable as a physics-grounded reference generator for ep18 and ep24, but only in a configuration the authors did not report. It needs:
- OBJECT_FLOOR_COLLISION=True, USE_PEDESTAL=False and USE_SUPPORT=False set in launch.py;
- floor_penalty_scale=0.5 and pedestal_penalty_scale=0;
- thicken=0 and dilate=0, so its hulls match ours;
- add_ur3_arm=False, since our hands float;
- our own CoACD pieces.
Our rest poses must be repaired first. With 1-4 cm floats the floor gate never arms, and the default pedestal would just hold the object at the wrong height. The published 0.71 includes the pedestal and plate mechanism, so it says nothing about the floor-only setup we would use. Its 'Object Base' sizing rule is worth reusing for our support patch, but for us the patch has to be real mesh, because the evaluator has no collision-only geoms.

checked.what_source_actually_says:
In v1/v2, every quoted figure is present as stated: about 18% over annealed-only sampling, 3.0 vs 2.5 Hz, 0.479/1022 and 0.879/756, 47.9% vs 39.5%, 42.0% vs 67.1%, H = 1.2 s, N = 1024, M = 16, 5 seeds. v2 also describes residual RL on SPIDER's nominal controls, and the contact filter (contacts shorter than t_c,min or drifting more than d_c,max are disabled).
v3 (26 Sep 2026) reports ADD-AUC on a 0-10 cm scale instead of success rates:
- vs ManipTrans (Isaac Gym, Oakink, Inspire hand): 19.4 vs SPIDER 22.3;
- vs DexMachina (Genesis, ARCTIC): 41.0 vs SPIDER 43.6, so SPIDER is no longer worse on ARCTIC;
- kinematic vs SPIDER vs SPIDER with contact guidance: Oakink 11.0 / 48.8 / 56.9; GigaHands 24.1 / 56.9 / 57.7.
v3 runs Allegro, XHand, Inspire and Sharpa 'without contact guidance, since noisy demonstrations can make contact signals unreliable'. In its RL experiments the SPIDER feedforward is used to initialise policy targets and as the tracking reference: 'we do not use it as a residual control term'. v3 adds automatic retries that boost the per-step budget on hard segments. Its paper dataset is 6,520 trajectories (14,604 eligible rollouts, ADD-AUC 61.67% before filtering). The README separately reports the 2026-09-22 Hugging Face release of 7,876 successful right-hand trajectories covering Allegro, XHand, Inspire and Sharpa, so that figure is correct.

Supplementary C.1: interaction is assumed if any of these holds:
(i) the object is airborne and its acceleration deviates significantly from g;
(ii) it is on the ground, not static, and its acceleration is not explained by friction alone;
(iii) the minimum human-object vertex distance is under 0.01 m.
The contact threshold is then that minimum distance + 0.005 m. Neutral zones neither promote nor penalise contact. PSI keeps a FIFO buffer of reference states and high-reward simulated states. Interaction early termination fires when a required contact is lost for more than 10 consecutive frames, or when the object or hand-object distance deviates by more than 0.5 m.

sources.method:
Each object is a union of convex hulls. Hull vertices and object poses are optimised jointly with an augmented-Lagrangian / Levenberg–Marquardt solver. Terms:
- trimmed-ICP-like fit to the observed points, plus shape regularisers;
- static force equilibrium with friction;
- a collision potential from the shape-differentiable SDRS separating-plane contact model.

'Separating-plane aggregation' uses one plane per interacting object pair. This explicitly regularises occluded contact regions to be flat and aligned with the partner or support. Initialisation comes from SAM3D + FoundationPose.

sources.evidence:
5 tabletop scenes, up to 5 objects / 22 hulls, checked in MuJoCo:
- max drift over 1 min: 0.73–3.10 cm vs 31–173 cm for the SAM3D+FoundationPose start;
- max kinetic energy in 1 s: 7e-4–1.1e-2 J vs 2.1–5.7 J;
- PSNR comparable (≈17–22 dB).

Wall time on CPU is 46–540 min per scene.

sources.adoption_effort:
Full reimplementation is not feasible in 13 days. A light variant takes about 2–3 days on CPU:
- keep our tracked poses (small corrections only);
- move only hull vertices in voxels our TSDF never observed;
- check equilibrium by a MuJoCo settle test rather than by the ALM.

sources.method:
A post-hoc optimisation over all object poses with three costs:
- a pose cost that stays close to the initial estimates;
- a collision cost: a hinge on negative signed distances between convex-decomposition pieces (differentiable GJK, DiffCol);
- a gravity cost that pulls each object along gravity to its nearest static support, switched off once the object touches something.

The table plane is estimated from two views with MASt3R + RANSAC.

sources.evidence:
YCB-Video BOP average recall:
- known scene: MegaPose 0.620 → 0.706, FoundPose 0.690 → 0.774;
- table estimated with a support view: 0.650 / 0.715;
- single-view scene estimate degrades results (0.597 / 0.658).

The ablation shows each of the three terms is needed. The authors also report improved real pick-and-place success.

sources.method:
Single RGB-D human video → MANO hands (WiLoR) and object poses (FoundationPose with given 3D models). Then:
- task-aware pose refinement at assembly: peg–hole axis co-linearity plus an endpoint relative-position loss;
- residual RL in IsaacLab with temporal segments, success-gated resets and an event-driven reward curriculum, for bimanual assembly.

ideas:
H8 (interaction graph from tracks, S1, to choose which constraints apply where; no VLM). Edge rules:
- relative pose static and surfaces within 5 mm → contact;
- one object below the other along gravity → support;
- overlap above 0 at rest → nested / inserted;
- the pair moves together rigidly → attached.

Test: the graph agrees with manual video labels on at least 11 of the 12 two-object public episodes. Falsified if thresholds need per-episode tuning.
